    "slug": "analysts-undermine-somali-resilience-fuel-al-shabaab-narrative-for-over-a-decade",
    "summary": "A new report details how security analysts, both in Somalia and abroad, have consistently downplayed Somali forces' capabilities and amplified Al-Shabaab propaganda, influencing public perception and policy.",
    "content": "For more than ten years, certain security analysts based inside and outside Somalia have systematically underestimated the resilience of the Somali people and the capacity of the Somali National Armed Forces. Their narratives have often, whether intentionally or not, reinforced the messaging of the militant group Al-Shabaab, predicting on multiple occasions that Mogadishu would fall under the group's control.\n\nA prominent example came during the 2010 Ramadan offensive, a significant military campaign launched by Al-Shabaab. Reports published at the time and circulated by various media outlets echoed the group's claims, portraying the offensive as a major threat to the capital. This pattern of commentary, the report argues, has had tangible economic and political consequences, as well as serving as a form of psychological warfare against the Somali public.\n\nThe continued dissemination of such narratives undermines the morale of Somali security forces and emboldens militant groups by amplifying their perceived strength. For the Somali diaspora, who rely on both local and international media to stay informed, these skewed perspectives can distort the reality on the ground and influence perceptions of Somalia's stability and progress.\n\nThe report calls for a more balanced and evidence-based approach to security analysis, one that recognizes the achievements and sacrifices of Somali forces rather than uncritically accepting Al-Shabaab's propaganda.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/27/buunbuuninta-al-shabaab-dano-dhaqaale-siyaasadeed-iyo-dagaal-nafsi-ah/",
