    "summary": "The Somali Future Council, comprising Puntland, Jubbaland, and opposition groups, has urged international partners to prioritize political consensus over security arrangements in Somalia's transition.",
    "content": "On July 27, 2026, the Somali Future Council—an alliance of Puntland, Jubbaland, and opposition factions—sent an open letter to leaders of nations contributing troops to the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM). The letter expresses gratitude to Uganda, Kenya, Ethiopia, Djibouti, Burundi, and Egypt for their military contributions but calls for a unified political solution to precede any security transitions.\n\nThe council's request comes amid ongoing disagreements over Somalia's electoral model and federal-state relations. Puntland and Jubbaland have long advocated for a decentralized governance structure, while the central government in Mogadishu favors a more centralized approach. The letter suggests that without a political settlement, security gains could be undermined.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development underscores the persistent fragmentation within Somali politics. Many diaspora members have invested in rebuilding efforts but remain concerned about political instability. The council's appeal may signal a potential shift in how international actors engage with Somalia, possibly leading to a new transitional framework.\n\nThe signatories emphasize that a holistic political agreement is necessary before any changes to AUSSOM's mandate or troop levels. They argue that focusing solely on security without addressing political grievances risk repeating past cycles of conflict.\n\nAs the international community considers future engagement, the council's letter could influence donor priorities and peacebuilding strategies. The diaspora, which often serves as a bridge between Somali communities and global partners, may find itself playing a key role in advocating for inclusive political processes.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/27/puntland-jubbaland-iyo-mucaaradka-oo-beesha-caalamka-ka-dalbaday-in-soomaaliya-la-geliyo-hannaan-kumeelgaarka-ah/",
